~ start
Welcome back, valued Employee!
Are you ready for another fulfilling day?
...
That’s what I like to hear!
$> InitDishes.create_sloth()
Oh! A customer!
Go see what they’d like!
$> InitDishes.tutorial_marker += 1
=> END

~ sloth_interact
Did you catch all of that?
Go ahead and open your ordering tablet! (Press "Tab" or click the icon in the top right)
=> END

~ tablet_opened
Wow! I never realized we had so many items on the menu!
Seems like we’ll need to filter through our items.
Go ahead and click on the “Filter” function on your tablet!
=> END

~ tablet_filter
What was it the customer wanted again?
Something red, handheld, and meaty?
=> END

~ wrong_filter1
That doesn't seem quite right.
Wasn't it something red, handheld, and meaty?
=> END

~ chicken
Oh! Looks like the customer wants fried chicken!
Go ahead and order it, and the chefs’ll set it out as fast as possible!
=> END

~ served1
Another customer is satisfied!
$> InitDishes.create_pig()
Speaking of another customer, looks like there’s someone new!
=> END

~ pig_interact
Wow, this customer can’t seem to make up their mind!
I bet they don’t even want any of those, but I’m sure we can figure out what they’re craving.
Let’s take another look at the ordering tablet!
$> InitDishes.tutorial_marker += 1
=> END

~ search
Use the search bar to find the pepperoni pizza, escargot, and shivit oshi the customer asked for! Do make sure to note what tags are on each item!
=> END

~ search_done
Pepperoni pizza is yellow, handheld, and meaty. Escargot is green, handheld, and fishy. Shivit oshi is green, pasta, and meaty.
Looking at the overlapping tags here, I think the customer wants something green, handheld, and meaty! And I think we have just the item! Let's use the filter again.
=> END

~ wrong_filter2
That doesn't seem quite right.
Wasn't it something green, handheld, and meaty?
=> END

~ finish
Way to go, champ!
Seems like you’re ready to take on the day all by yourself!
I’m sure you’ll figure it out if anyone new comes by!
Best of luck!
$> InitDishes.start_game()
